VT_TALK("sql/sql-programmable", {
 "triggers": "A trigger is a small program stored inside the database that runs by itself whenever rows are inserted, updated or deleted. Inside the trigger, NEW means the row after the change, and OLD means the row before. A BEFORE trigger can check values and refuse a change with an error. An AFTER trigger is perfect for writing an audit log, so every price change is recorded without any app having to remember. In the animation, raise a price and watch the log fill, then try a negative stock and watch the guard trigger refuse it. Triggers fire once for every changed row. Think of a door that rings a bell every time it opens.",
 "uses": "Triggers are good at keeping copies of data correct. Store an item count on each order, and an AFTER INSERT trigger on order lines adds each new quantity, so the total can never drift. An INSTEAD OF trigger tells SQLite what an update or delete on a view should really do, which makes a read only view writable. Use triggers for audit logs, history and stored totals, but keep them short and documented, because the app code cannot see them, and a trigger that fires another trigger becomes hard to follow. Think of an automatic counter at a stadium gate that clicks once for every person.",
 "generated": "A generated column is worked out from other columns of the same row, such as a line total from quantity times price. You never write it yourself, so it can never be wrong. A VIRTUAL generated column is computed each time the row is read and takes no disk space. A STORED one is computed when the row is written and saved, which makes it cheap to read and index. If you try to write a value into a generated column, the database refuses. SQLite, PostgreSQL and MySQL all support them. Think of a spreadsheet cell holding a formula, which updates whenever its inputs change.",
 "procs": "Server databases can store whole programs. A stored procedure runs a sequence of statements, such as a transfer, when called with CALL or EXEC. A user defined function returns a value and can be used inside a query. PostgreSQL uses PL pg SQL, SQL Server uses T SQL, and Oracle uses PL SQL. SQLite runs inside your app, so it has no procedures in SQL, and the app registers extra functions in its own language. Keeping logic in the database guards the data for every app and saves round trips, while keeping it in the app is easier to test and move between engines. Think of a recipe card kept in the kitchen versus one each cook carries in their pocket.",
 "levels": "Up the ladder once more. For a beginner, triggers react to changes, generated columns are formulas, and procedures are saved programs. At college level, know when BEFORE, AFTER and INSTEAD OF triggers fire, and the difference between virtual and stored columns. At masters level, triggers run inside the same transaction as the statement, so a failing trigger undoes it, and some engines have statement level triggers. At research level, active databases and change data capture stream every change to other systems. Think of a smoke alarm wired to call the fire station."
});
